#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:5
#: e3ccea4652334b5a90932a1ae4879d6c
msgid "Glossary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:9
#: da2c4a03b1c9409e8054e9de82241f78
msgid "The glossary is not indexed. It is in alphabetical order so just scroll down to find the topic of interest."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:12
#: 9c5242ac0e03467b91209ca05766daa1
msgid "**2.4 Ghz**: The frequency used by digital (spread spectrum) radio communications in our applications, including 2.4Ghz RC, Bluetooth and some video transmission equipment. This is a different band than the older 72 Mhz band that is used for analog RC communications. To avoid radio frequency conflict is it often a good idea to use 72 Mhz radio equipment when you are using 2.4 Ghz onboard video transmitters, or use 900 Mhz video when using 2.4 Ghz RC equipment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:20
#: 879313cd00a046f995f3a88d7f772305
msgid "**AGL**: Altitude above ground level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:22
#: 295d39198fe749c6b595005f8174bffc
msgid "**AHRS**: Attitude Heading Reference System. An IMU (see below) plus the code to interpret the output from its sensors to establish a plane's XYZ and heading orientation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:26
#: 63c64a56cc70445eb11dca8c5becad98
msgid "**AMA**: Academy of Model Aeronautics. The main US model aircraft association. The AMA works closely with the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) to establish\\ `reasonable rules <https://www.modelaircraft.org/aboutama/gov.aspx>`__\\ for the use of amateur UAVs. Each AMA chapter and field may have slightly different policies, but it's possible to fly and test air frames and some technology on AMA fields without violating the association's (or FAA/NAS) rules."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:34
#: e5669c357b5340688850d488e81cc15b
msgid "**Arduino**: An open source embedded processor project. Includes a hardware standard currently based on the Atmel Atmega168 microprocessor and necessary supporting hardware, and a software programming environment based on the C-like Processing language. Official site is \\ `here <http://www.arduino.cc>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:40
#: bb55153a1b9048c7b21f991ac236c658
msgid "**APM (AutoPilot Mega)**: Autopilot providing capabilities for stabilized flight, position maintenance and automated mission (waypoint) path following."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:44
#: f4c208436fc942b598c2859489366df5
msgid "**ATC**: This could be the Atitude Control library in Copter with params starting with \"ATC\\_\" or it could mean Air Traffic Control. Usually located at an airport control tower but can also be an app or service such as \\ `AirMap <http://www.airmap.com>`__ or \\ `Altitude Angel <http://www.altitudeangel.com>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:46
#: 03bf355700ce408dbfeee6f49b8a24bb
msgid "**Autopilot**: A device that provides automatic vehicle control (stabilization, pilot commands via RC or GCS, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:48
#: 1c650d3fcbf2421caccf588a3dda48d6
msgid "**BEC**: Battery Elimination Circuit. A voltage regulator found in ESCs (see below) and as a stand-alone product. Designed to provide constant 5v voltage for RC equipment, autopilots and other onboard electronics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:52
#: d5d38584b83e44b1a704decfe8b2ecd2
msgid "**Blimp**: Floating aerial autopilot software variant of the ArduPilot project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:55
#: b7b6a67716784ffab4ee5bec7b1047b7
msgid "**Bootloader**: Special software code stored in non-volatile memory in a microprocessor that can interface with a PC to download a user's program. Also see Firmware."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:59
#: b2a4713cca5e45d3bf3b84cb60c9a5c0
msgid "**COA**: Certificate of Authorization. A FAA approval for a UAV flight. See the \\ `faa.gov <http://www.faa.gov/about/office_org/headquarters_offices/ato/service_units/systemops/aaim/organizations/uas/coa/>`__ web site for more details and the \\ `DIY Drones Regulatory FAQ <https://www.diydrones.com/profiles/blogs/regulatory-faq>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:63
#: fc0aff693daf4eaf9e762ef2fd09d8c6
msgid "**Copter**: Rotary-wing autopilot software variant of the ArduPilot project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:66
#: ddc0d4588ad14d0bbf7a0d6794fa1fe0
msgid "**DCM**: Discrete Cosine Matrix. A algorithm that is a less processing intensive equivalent of the Kalman Filter. See \\ `this <https://diydrones.com/forum/topics/robust-estimator-of-the>`__ for more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:71
#: 11983e38eb784367838e1253bbbcaaae
msgid "**Eagle file**: The schematic and PCB design files (and related files that tell PCB fabs how to create the boards) generated by the free \\ `Cadsoft Eagle <http://www.cadsoftusa.com/>`__ program. This is the most common standard used in the open source hardware world, although, ironically, it's not open source software itself. Needless to say, this is not optimal, and the Eagle software is clumsy and hard to learn. One hopes that an open source alternative will someday emerge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:79
#: b7310006dc2a4d8e86e8ca1011e1f452
msgid "**ESC**: Electronic Speed Control. Device to control the motor in an electric aircraft. Serves as the connection between the main battery and the RC receiver. Usually includes a BEC, or Battery Elimination Circuit, which provides power for the RC system and other onboard electronics, such as an autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:85
#: 3abaa57618804f919509b9aaf8305aaa
msgid "**Firmware**: The software or sketch that is loaded into the non-volatile memory of microprocessor based products. It is called 'firmware' because it stays in the non-volatile memory even if power is removed - thus 'non' - volatile. In the case of the autopilots, it is the 'program' or application (App to smart phone users) that determines what the auto pilot does, and how."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:92
#: 3bddb3c218a34d878efec4548e9e452a
msgid "**FPV**: First-person view. A technique that uses an onboard video camera and wireless connection to the ground allow a pilot on the ground with video goggles to fly with a cockpit view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:96
#: ac012f5e8bca42fb852bbe9ab344dff4
msgid "**FTDI**: A standard to convert USB to serial communications. Available as a chip for boards that have a USB connector, or as an \\ `adapter <https://www.amazon.com/ftdi-adapter/s?k=ftdi+adapter>`__ to connected to breakout pins. FTDI stands for Future Technology Devices International, which is the name of the company that makes the chips."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:102
#: cbb911d4da944ebc973ff1bca53be835
msgid "**GCS**: Ground Control Station. Software running on a computer on the ground that receives telemetry information from an airborne UAV and displays its progress and status, often including video and other sensor data. Can also be used to transmit in-flight commands to the UAV."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:107
#: 629fb6cd8aeb44a2a1d4b85640869cd6
msgid "**Hard Iron magnetic distortion**: distortions that arise from magnets or magnetized metals on the airframe that affect the compass. These distortions remain in the same location relative to the compass for all heading orientations. These distortions can be mostly compensated for by adding constant offsets to the values returned from the 3d magnetometer (compass)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:114
#: 658de1b72f5c44d69b050a76c13dbe87
msgid "**HIL (Hardware-in-the-loop simulation)**: Doing a simulation where software running on another computer generates data that simulates the data that would be coming from an autopilot's sensors. The autopilot is running and doesn't \"know\" that the data is simulated, so it responds just as it would to real sensor data. Hardware-in-the-loop uses the physical autopilot hardware connected to a simulator, as opposed to simulating the autopilot in software, too. In an \"open loop\" simulation, the software simulator feeds data to the hardware autopilot; in a \"closed loop\" simulation, the hardware feeds data back to the software simulator, too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:125
#: 49695d636cb4460b9834fe45e8815d03
msgid "**I2C**: A serial bus that allows multiple low speed peripherals, such as sensors, to be connected to a microprocessor. See \\ `this <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%C2%B2C>`__ for more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:129
#: ff06f1566f9b44078df48950c3a55ce0
msgid "**IDE**: An integrated development environment, such as the \\ `Arduino <https://www.arduino.cc/en/Main/Software>`__ editor/downloader/serial monitor software. Often includes a debugger."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:133
#: a631559e60ad482a9283a8ca4fe8c616
msgid "**IMU**: An inertial measurement unit such as the ArduPilot OilPan. Usually has at least three accelerometers (measuring the gravity vector in the x,y and z dimensions) and two gyros (measuring rotation around the tilt and pitch axis). Neither are sufficient by themselves, since accelerometers are thrown off by movement (ie, they are \"noisy\" over short periods of time), while gyros drift over time. The data from both types of sensors must be combined in software to determine true aircraft attitude and movement to create an AHRS (see above). One technique for doing this is the Kalman filter (see below). Now superseded by Autopilots which include CPU and sensors in one unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:145
#: 741a236539494c40ab640545a28c53e3
msgid "**Inner loop/Outer loop**: Usually used to refer to the stabilization and navigation functions of an autopilot. The stabilization function must run in real-time and as often as 100 times a second (\"inner loop\"), while the navigation function can run as infrequently as once per second and can tolerate delays and interruptions (\"outer loop\")."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:151
#: f8d551dd0ac547ce9ecd84f4337ae227
msgid "**INS**: Inertial Navigation System. A way to calculate position based on an initial GPS reading followed by readings from motion and speed sensors using dead reckoning. Useful when GPS is not available or has temporarily lost its signal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:156
#: c219b5836ecc49f7b7bc3cd09d439e56
msgid "**ICSP: In Circuit Serial Programmer**. A way to load code to a microprocessor. Usually seen as a six-pin (two rows of three) connector on a PCB. To use this, you need a programmer, such as this one, that uses the SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface) standard."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:161
#: ed544cd395d14536b2ae679f4f452441
msgid "**Kalman Filter**: A relatively complicated algorithm that, in our applications, is primarily used to combine accelerometer and gyro data to provide an accurate description of aircraft attitude and movement in real time. See \\ `this <http://tom.pycke.be/mav/71/kalman-filtering-of-imu-data>`__ for more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:168
#: 24ad86d993914dccb87837ddfe70daf0
msgid "**LOS**: Line of Sight. See VLOS below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:170
#: 1b3f28e4503343ce8364bb9e258ddd48
msgid "**LiPo**: Lithium Polymer battery, aka LiPoly. Variants include Lithium Ion (Li-Ion) battery. This battery chemistry offers more power and lighter weight than NiMh and NiCad batteries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:174
#: 9c4928643ef24aa0a22a00ddc525fd9a
msgid "**MAVLink**. The Micro Air Vehicle communications protocol used by the Copter and Plane line of autopilots. See \\ `here <http://qgroundcontrol.org/mavlink/start>`__ for more info on MAVLink."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:179
#: d2ecbe32fa1a42b88e269456bdc4e2b0
msgid "**MAV**: Micro Air Vehicle. A small UAV. More \\ `here <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Micro_air_vehicle>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:182
#: dc07a451a39240438bd0e1c862ad1a6c
msgid "**NMEA**: National Marine Electronics Association standard for GPS information. When we refer to \"NMEA sentences\", we're talking about ASCII strings from a GPS module that look like this: $GPGGA,123519,4807.038,N,01131.000,E,1,08,0.9,545.4,M,46.9,M,,\\ **47**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:187
#: 672093424d404c42a046218cc4688fa1
msgid "**Oilpan**: An arduino style shield meant to be paired with the ArduPilotMega. Contained the majority of the sensors (gyos, accelerometers, barometer, etc) that allow the ArduPilotMega to function as an autopilot, now superseded by subsequent Autopilots that incorporate CPU and sensors in one board."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:193
#: f96f6d8b71ee471189b47606dbccede3
msgid "**OSD**: On-screen display. A way to integrate data (often telemetry information) into the real-time video stream the aircraft is sending to the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:197
#: 575c1a5281ce4534960110d2c6926668
msgid "**Plane**: Fixed-wing autopilot software variant of the ArduPilot project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:200
#: 93fff6c6ef904e6c8c6c240bf41c034d
msgid "**PCB**: Printed circuit board. In our use, a specialized board designed and \"fabbed\" for a dedicated purpose, as opposed to a breadboard or prototype board, which can be used and reused for many projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:204
#: 14bc929078104ad1bd79344d1b7ac5a9
msgid "**PCM**: Pulse Code Modulation: The technique some modern RC transmitters/receivers communicate the RC channels information. It is transmitted digitally using a digital code to represent the channel number, position of the transmitter stick or switch, and sometimes other information. Some modern RC systems also provide digital signals to control digital servos - typically specific to each manufacturer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:211
#: 511ac6add95d4fea95cf5edc8c1d8925
msgid "**PDB**: Power Distribution Board. A board used in multicopters to distribute power to multiple ESCs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:214
#: c8522e7f49734b6a92f9f45bcec6b512
msgid "**PIC**: Pilot in Command. Refers to a FAA requirement that UAVs stay under a pilot's direct control if they are flying under the recreational exemption to COA approval. See Line of Sight above. (Not to be confused with the PIC processor series by Microchip)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:219
#: 05479edde8a2406f936e120569ed716d
msgid "**PID**: Proportional/Integral/Derivative control method. A machine control algorithm that allows for more accurate sensor-motion control loops and less overcontrol. See \\ `this <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PID_controller>`__ for more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:224
#: c8c521790d8748c1bef0764c644d2655
msgid "**POI**: Point of Interest. Designates a spot that a UAV should keep a camera pointed towards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:226
#: c2ee6a9b5ec6463bb30be77718bd555f
msgid "**PPM**: `Pulse Position Modulation <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pulse-position_modulation>`__. The square-wave pulse train used to transmit multiple channels of information between some RC transmitters and receivers. Some RC receivers provide a PPM output (sometimes referred to as the PPM Sum) which can be used on Pixhawk-series boards. Other receivers convert the PPM signal and only provide PWM signals for each channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:233
#: e4fd1a4c8e2b4d6da4abd0ed604430b0
msgid "**PWM**: Pulse Width Modulation. The square-wave signals used in RC control to drive servos and speed controllers. There is one PWM signal for each channel. The width varies from 1000 to 2000 microseconds, depending on the RC manufacturer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:237
#: 223b4f474fb24b5284912f06d4c8b9af
msgid "**PX4FMU/PX4IO**: First version of Pixhawk-series autopilots (FMUv1). Discontinued."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:239
#: ec27cc8c2a1c42f7b08954a6fe172333
msgid "**Rover**: Ground and water autopilot software variant of the ArduPilot project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:242
#: a062e0562e164e6db52f3f9247c4e3d7
msgid "**RTL**: Return to Launch. Fly back to the \"home\" location where the aircraft took off."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:244
#: 8905ec514e3f40f3a260583464f46886
msgid "**SiRF III**: The standard used by most modern GPS modules. Includes !SiRF III binary mode, which is an alternative to the ASCII-based NMEA standard described above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:248
#: ace28c63a0d44b368a26e92d80fd477f
msgid "**Sketch**: The program files, drivers and other code generated by the Arduinio IDE for a single project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:251
#: 4c493728e18d42dd92eeb272cf2b487b
msgid "**SVN**: Short for the Subversion version-control repository used by the DIY Drones and other teams for source code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:254
#: ec9c4edb2e4a456e8b1c954338b4082a
msgid "**Sub**: Underwater autopilot software variant of the ArduPilot project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:256
#: a7f5282a209f4c7083809249080e4c59
msgid "**Telemetry System**: A two way radio system to allow flight data to be sent from your aircraft and also to allow control or adjustment information to be sent back to it from a \"ground station\", commonly a laptop computer. See the :ref:`telemetry landing page <common-telemetry-landingpage>` for options."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:261
#: 6c17a5d534cb460ea8b3f58d1a1a52c3
msgid "**Thermopile**: An infrared detector. Often used in pairs in UAVs to measure tilt and pitch by looking at differences in the infrared signature of the horizon fore and aft and on both sides. This is based on the fact that there is always an infrared gradient between earth and sky, and that you can keep a plane flying level by ensuring that the readings are the same from both sensors in each pair, each looking in opposite directions. These have been largely displaced by electronic gyros and are no longer in common use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:270
#: e2eff053f68a48f0916fe3c5478239a0
msgid "**UAV**: Unmanned Aerial Vehicle. In the military, these are increasingly called Unmanned Aerial Systems (UAS), to reflect that the aircraft is just part of a complex system in the air and on the ground. Ground-based autonomous robots are called Unmanned Ground Vehicles (UGVs) and robot submersibles are called Autonomous Underwater Vehicles (AUVs). Robot boats are called Unmanned Surface Vehicles (USVs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:277
#: 16df7ad9a3df4db2a19aaf663a83262e
msgid "**VLOS**: Visual Line of Sight. The pilot's ability to see an aircraft from the ground well enough to control it, without the use of artificial visual aids (aside from glasses). Required by FAA regs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:281
#: 6e94ca5ebd324d76b618e72130938693
msgid "**WAAS**: Wide Area Augmentation System. A system of satellites and ground stations that provide GPS signal corrections, giving up to five times better position accuracy than uncorrected GPS. See \\ `this <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wide_Area_Augmentation_System>`__ for more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:287
#: 16932368c9ae40bc8285159caa502088
msgid "**Xbee**: The commercial name of the recommend ZigBee-compatible radio modems commonly used by amateur UAVs. They can be found at `sparkfun <https://www.sparkfun.com/products/10421>`__ although remember you also need a board to mount it on like this one from \\ `sparkfun <https://www.sparkfun.com/products/11812>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-glossary.rst:293
#: 94dba73abe804a72aaf5b2a43be51d5a
msgid "**ZigBee**: A wireless communications standard, which has longer range than Bluetooth but lower power consumption than WiFi."
msgstr ""
